00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.410
So, fungible,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:44.560
let's talk about the word fungible.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.240
It's this very specific word

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:47.230
that economists use,

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.460
it has a very precise definition.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:52.020
I wanna use a different word
for fungible for a second.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.010
Let's just use the word replaceable.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.530
Non-fungible means non-replaceable,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.410
you can't replace it, there's
only one of them, it's unique.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:00.400
Non fungible.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.360
Let me give you an example

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.360
of something I feel very strongly about.

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:10.233
Let's say you want to
buy an orange jacket.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.540
This is really absurd,

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.673
I've actually never counted these before.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.390
- Stop it, get some help.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.640
- You wanna buy an orange
jacket from Uni Qlo.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.400
You go on the internet,
and a jacket costs $39.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.050
If you purchase one of
these jackets for $39,

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:33.820
you don't care what specific
jacket they send to you,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.840
they're gonna make thousands
of jackets in your size,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.610
send them to stores, send them to people,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.910
and they will send one to you,

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:41.000
you don't care which one it is.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.560
The jacket is fungible, it's replaceable.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.210
As long as you get one
that's identical to the rest,

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.670
it's worth the same to you,
they're interchangeable.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:53.120
However, let's talk about
one Uni Qlo orange jacket

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.033
that has been with me
for a very long time.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:00.100
This is the original,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.170
and for those of you who don't know

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.100
I sort of have a strange
attachment to this jacket.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.360
I just love it, I love the color,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:07.840
I feel like an identity with this thing.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.500
And it's sort of starting to disintegrate,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.333
but I love it.

00:05:11.333 --> 00:05:12.560
And I kind of fell in love.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.260
This jacket is not replaceable.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.470
If I went onto the website and paid $39

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.820
for a Uni Qlo orange jacket

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.020
that was this exact same model,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.070
it would not be this jacket.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.560
This jacket is non-fungible.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.150
It is the only one on
the planet that exists.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.981
It has emotional value.

00:05:29.981 --> 00:05:31.960
It has significance.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:34.610
It is a very valuable
thing because it is scarce.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.040
There is only one of them,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.050
it's valuable to me at least.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.130
And I kind of fell in love.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.673
Okay, we can put these down for a second.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:44.960
Everything in our economy
is one or the other,

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.400
fungible or non-fungible.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.970
A sack or rice is fungible,

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:48.890
you just want a sack of rice,

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.030
you don't care which one it is.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.040
The Mona Lisa, non-fungible,
there's only one.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.330
Unsurprisingly, non-fungible things

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.970
are way more valuable
than fungible things.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:01.210
To that's the NF in NFT, non-fungible.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:04.620
Now let's talk about
the T, which is token.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.830
This is a very internet-y word.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.750
And to explain this, I
have to explain something

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.313
I have avoided explaining
for a very long time,

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.470
the blockchain.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.600
Luckily, there's a way to understand this,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.510
and I'm going to make it
as painless as possible.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:25.100
Let's say I want to buy
three slices of pizza

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.800
from my friend Anna.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:29.380
She charges me six dollars
for these three slices.

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:30.460
I don't use cash anymore,

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.070
so I pull out my debit card, my bank card,

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:35.620
and I swipe on her little terminal.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.030
As soon as I swipe this card
a message is sent to my bank

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.840
and it says hey, Johnny, who
has an account at your bank

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.460
wants to spend six dollars on pizza,

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:47.120
and that money needs to go to Anna's bank.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.410
This is like the bread and
butter of what a bank does,

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.770
all day, they document every transaction

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.330
that comes in from all their customers,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.480
they send out money to the other banks,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.500
and at the end of the day

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.190
they have a tally of all the money

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.830
that went out of your
account and into your account

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:02.970
and they can give you a number.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.160
They can say based on all
of these transactions,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.460
you have $50 in your bank account.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.270
And so when that request
comes in as I swipe my card

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.790
my bank is like okay,

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.470
based on all of your transactions

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:14.800
you have $50 in your account,

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.150
I can send six dollars
to Anna's bank, approved.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.040
And they approve the transaction.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.290
Once that money comes into Anna's bank,

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:24.090
Anna's bank is doing the same thing.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.880
They're like oh cool, she had $80

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:26.747
and now she has $86,

00:07:26.747 --> 00:07:28.920
and they add it to her record.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.040
More and more your money is
just a number on a screen.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:36.640
It's the result of a
bunch of transactions.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.670
You don't barter with physical things,

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.950
you don't use cash as much.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:43.340
So the bank keeping meticulous records

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:46.560
of every transaction
becomes really important.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.510
We trust the bank to do this correctly.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:50.800
So thank you banks.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.150
Banks and other middle men

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.580
have been keeping stuff
like this running smoothly

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.847
for centuries.

00:07:55.847 --> 00:07:58.230
I mean kind of smoothly.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:07:59.780
- [Reporter] The NASDAQ,
everything and more

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:00.904
has been completely wiped out.

00:08:00.904 --> 00:08:02.270
- [Reporter] It was the
worst day on Wall Street.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:03.870
- What in the world is
happening on Wall Street?

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.330
- There have been a few bumps in the road.

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.550
With the rise of the internet,
people started to wonder.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.470
Is there a way that we
could do this same thing,

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:10.870
coordinate this same transaction

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:15.480
of transfer of money between
two people without the bank?

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:20.480
The result is a very clever
concept called the blockchain.

00:08:20.617 --> 00:08:21.450
The blockchain.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:24.930
The blockchain fulfills the
same thing the bank was doing,

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:27.470
but instead of doing this
privately on my bank account

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:29.270
and talking to Anna's bank,

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.960
all of the transactions
are actually recorded

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.958
publicly on the internet.

00:08:33.958 --> 00:08:36.360
♪ You're going surfing on the internet ♪

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.370
So let's redo this
example in a crypto world.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.710
Anna charges me six crypto coins

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:43.540
for my three slices of pizza.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:46.360
I go to swipe my proverbial bank card

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.140
to say yes, I want to pay you six coins.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.777
Instead of the bank seeing
that request for a transaction

00:08:52.777 --> 00:08:54.720
and trying to validate it,

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.850
it goes on to this public record

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.440
where a bunch of people's computers

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.520
all around the world are keeping track

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:04.572
of every single transaction
of everyone always.

00:09:04.572 --> 00:09:07.930
If I don't indeed have the
six coins in my account

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.370
to pay Anna,

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:10.820
all of the people's computers

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.440
who are keeping track of
every single transaction

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.600
will notice that there's a discrepancy.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:16.990
They'll be like whoa whoa whoa dude,

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:19.130
you don't have six coins.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:20.910
We're looking at every transaction ever

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:22.450
and you don't have six coins.

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.340
Your transaction is rejected.

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.810
If I do have six coins,

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.760
all of the computers
looking at the public record

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:31.060
will see that request for a transaction

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:32.570
and they'll be like yep, approved.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.510
You have six coins and
now Anna has six coins.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:38.930
And they'll write that transaction
into the public record.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.020
Now Anna having those six extra coins

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.480
is now the business of everybody,

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:44.760
everybody now knows that.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.490
The point here is that the group verifies

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.110
the legitimacy of every transaction

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.530
by keeping an eye on every transaction

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.080
to make sure that it adds up.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.940
Okay I'm getting hot at this point,

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.870
so I'm taking off my orange jacket.

00:09:56.870 --> 00:09:58.730
Okay, so you're wondering

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.470
what does the blockchain
and this public record

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.690
have anything to do with cat gifs

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:06.633
that sell for $600,000?

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:09.520
Well I'm about to tell you.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.280
So in my pizza example we
talked about blockchain

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.480
as a way to verify currency transaction.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.410
I pay you this much, you pay me this much

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.740
and everybody knows how much everybody has

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.160
because it's all public.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:22.250
But this is where it starts

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.380
to bend my mind a little bit,

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:24.840
what if we apply this to something

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.730
that isn't money or currency?

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.627
Let's say one day you're
just looking at the ledger

00:10:29.627 --> 00:10:30.700
and the ledger's like

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:33.890
Johnny wants to give Anna six coins.

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.790
Okay he's got six coins, approved.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:36.940
And then a transaction comes up

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:38.920
that's like a Malaysian businessman

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:41.160
wants to give three million dollars

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.300
worth of coins to Jack Dorsey

00:10:43.300 --> 00:10:46.290
in exchange for a little token,

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:48.560
or digital certificate that says

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.670
that the tweet is now somehow owned

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:52.870
by the Malaysian businessman.

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:56.080
The only thing that the
blockchain cares about

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.270
is does the Malaysian businessman

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:00.610
have three million dollars worth of coins?

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:02.610
And so a bunch of computers
all around the world

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.740
look at the whole entire
list of transactions

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.220
and say like yeah, this guy has more

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:10.820
than three million dollars
worth of coins, approved.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:12.270
They approve the transaction,

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.380
and now it is written in a public record

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:16.570
that is unalterable

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:21.640
that says that this Malaysian
businessman owns this tweet.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.490
The token has been
transferred to somebody new,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.440
non-fungible token, NFT.

00:11:27.826 --> 00:11:29.860
And if there's anything
that gets human psychology

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.560
to value something,

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:35.130
it's if an entire group
validates that it's real

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:36.710
and that there's only one of them.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:40.930
There are tens of thousands
of NFTs of all kinds.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:44.380
Some music is being given tokens,

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:47.650
lots of art is being minted as tokens

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:49.420
and being bought and sold,

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:50.720
and then of course there's.

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:51.870
- NBA Top Shot.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:53.350
- Who's taking advantage of this.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:54.340
- These highlight moments,

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:57.010
these Top Shot moments from
your favorite NBA players

00:11:57.010 --> 00:11:59.920
have been turned into non-fungible tokens.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:02.803
- Jesse made headlines the other day

00:12:02.803 --> 00:12:06.940
when he paid $208,000 for
a Lebron James Top Shot.

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:08.320
- It's the weirdest thing.

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:11.710
As soon as humans have enough abundance

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:12.930
to have their basic needs met,

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.440
food, shelter, warmth, et cetera,

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.950
the next frontier is to create value

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:21.450
in things that have no inherent value.

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:25.470
The value turns into psychological hype.

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:28.000
Excitement around a certain thing.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:28.833
We've been doing that forever,

00:12:28.833 --> 00:12:30.970
I mean the whole art industry is based

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.810
on the idea of a bunch of people

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.210
deciding that this painting,

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:37.740
this little bit of
canvas and wood and paint

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.913
is valuable, and thus it is valuable.

00:12:42.670 --> 00:12:43.630
The only different about now

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.270
is we now have the technology

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:48.460
to do this in a non-physical way

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:51.370
using this very sophisticated
internet technology

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.670
that is maturing very quickly.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.170
Okay, so this is a lot of hype,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.430
and I know you're thinking like cool,

00:12:57.430 --> 00:12:59.280
there's a bunch of rich people online

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.560
buying and trading digital art,

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.740
and there's millions of
dollars worth of cards,

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:04.670
I thought you said that this

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.950
was gonna have the potential
to change the world.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:08.840
And I'm getting there, but first

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.720
I need to talk about the crazy
flip side to the NFT fad.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:15.410
The reality is that the technology

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.290
that is the backbone for all of this,

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.740
the blockchain stuff that
we've been talking about

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.890
relies on the public ledger
thing that I talked about.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.250
That is the sort of heart
and soul conceptually,

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:27.250
but mechanically, like physically

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:29.440
what it relies on is computers

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:32.090
doing a bunch of little calculations

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:34.733
all day and night forever.

00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:37.500
These computers aren't real computers,

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.630
they don't have any memory,
or screens, or anything.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.200
All they do is just make
little micro-calculations

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.323
all day, all night.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:50.250
Most NFTs are stored on a
blockchain called Ethereum.

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:54.560
And as of now, in early April
2021 when I'm filming this,

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.130
the Ethereum blockchain

00:13:56.130 --> 00:14:00.420
is using 33 terawatt hours of electricity.

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.560
And you're like what's a
terawatt hour of electricity?

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.200
That's the same amount of power

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.720
as the country of Serbia.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:08.680
A reminder that generating electricity

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:09.900
usually comes from power plants

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.000
that are burning
fossil-fuels, that are putting

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:13.830
carbon into the atmosphere

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.740
which is a big freaking problem.

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:19.363
- [Narrator] Facing a manmade
disaster on a global scale.

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:22.410
- The power consumption
of the Ethereum blockchain

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:26.700
is exploding, it quadrupled
in the like eight months,

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.530
and it is showing no sign of slowing down.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:30.870
It is a lot of energy.

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.270
And to think that that much energy

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:35.130
is not being used to
like, move people around

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:36.500
or produce things,

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.290
it's used to crunch numbers

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.950
in a weird computer warehouse somewhere

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:43.890
so that somebody can buy a fake token

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.970
of a thing that we only,

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.450
oh man, I can't, it's mind blowing.

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.840
It's such an ironic moment

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:52.350
where it's like this is all digital,

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.630
it's all fake, it's not real,

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.763
but it's having deeply real world effects.

